VirtualObject::save writes presetName/vobName/visual twice on the unpacked
path, and omits the visual and ai objects the unpacked reader always expects.

`save`'s unpacked branch (`pack == false`) writes `presetName`, `vobName` and
`visual` itself, in the order `load`'s unpacked branch reads them. The common
tail below then writes all three a second time. ASCII and BinSafe entries are
read positionally, so the extras are a stream desync, not a harmless repeat -
and the two `visual` writes are not even the same value: the unpacked branch
wrote `visual_name`, which is empty on any VOB that was built rather than
loaded, where the packed tail writes `visual->name`. Both write `visual->name`
now.

The tail has the mirror-image hole for objects. `load` derives
`has_visual_object` and `has_ai_object` from `bit1`'s presence bits in the
packed form, but hardcodes both to `true` in the unpacked one - so an unpacked
VOB with no visual and no AI still has two objects read out of the stream,
while `save` wrote neither. Both are now written as the empty object (`% 0 0`),
which `ReadArchive::read_object` already returns null for.

Neither half is reachable upstream today: `VirtualObject.cc`'s file-static
`pack` is initialised to `true` and only `enable_packed_save` writes it, which
nothing calls. That is also why this could sit undetected - it is the unpacked
writer, not the ASCII one, so it is a defect of every archive format at once.
Requires 0045: `trafoOSToWSRot` is written on this same branch.

Upstreamable as-is: no API change, and it makes an existing public writer path
round-trip through the library's own reader.

